{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-120174",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/ddac2bab6fb72979e1142179dfb11792cc50044de545131139edfdeff9215e3c.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=\\pm 2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=4-{y}^{2}=(2+y)(2-y)\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}={y}^{2}-4=(y+2)(y-2).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=\\pm 2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-2$ | $-2<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\u2198$ | $\u2197$ | $\u2198$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=4-{y}^{2}=(2+y)(2-y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{\u2032}=4-{y}^{2}.$",
